  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Why do we cough?",
      "a": "Coughing is a reflex that clears mucus, dust, crumbs or germs from your throat and airways, keeping the path to your lungs open.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a cough for?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What sets off a cough?",
      "a": "Nerve sensors in the lining of the throat, windpipe and bronchi detect irritation, such as mucus, smoke or a crumb going the wrong way. They signal the brainstem to start the cough reflex.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about the airway sensors"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the glottis?",
      "a": "The glottis is the gap between your vocal cords in the voice box. It can close tight, which lets pressure build up in the chest before a cough or when you lift something heavy.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about the glottis"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How fast is the air in a cough?",
      "a": "The air in a cough can briefly reach many metres per second, fast enough to drag sticky mucus up the airways.",
      "asks": [
        "How strong is a cough?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Can you cough on purpose?",
      "a": "Yes. You can cough by choice, but the cough reflex can also happen without you deciding, when the airways are irritated.",
      "asks": [
        "Is coughing voluntary?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "A cough has three phases: a deep breath in, a compression phase with the glottis closed, and an explosive breath out.",
    "The cough reflex is coordinated in the brainstem."
  ],
